from .common import *
from .lg import S


def R(src, snk, bend=0.45):
    return A(src, snk, bend, rad=True)


def theory(B):
    B.h2("9.11  Electronic structure of carbon radicals: SOMO, spin density, EPR")
    B.ul(["<b>Orbital picture.</b> In a planar sp<sup>2</sup> alkyl radical the unpaired electron occupies a p orbital (the SOMO, singly occupied molecular orbital). The SOMO is higher than the filled bonding MOs and lower than the empty antibonding ones, so a radical can interact both with a donor LUMO-type partner and with an acceptor HOMO-type partner. A carbon radical with only three σ bonds is isoelectronic with the planar BH<sub>3</sub> framework plus one electron, and with the amine N–H inversion potential flattened (radical inversion barrier &lt; 1 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> for CH<sub>3</sub>•; ≈ 0.6 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> for t-Bu• to pyramidalise).",
          "<b>Pyramidalisation increases with electronegative and small substituents.</b> CH<sub>3</sub>• and alkyl radicals: planar or nearly so; •CF<sub>3</sub>: pyramidal (≈ 17° out-of-plane, inversion barrier ≈ 3 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>); •CH<sub>2</sub>F, •CHF<sub>2</sub> progressively pyramidal; cyclopropyl radical: pyramidal (the ring angle strain favours more p-character in the ring bonds and more s-character in the SOMO; barrier ≈ 3 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>); •CH<sub>2</sub>OH, •CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>: nearly planar with the lone pair donating into the SOMO (three-electron two-orbital interaction; the radical is stabilised by ≈ 5–7 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>).",
          "<b>σ-radicals</b> (vinyl, aryl, acyl, alkynyl) have the odd electron in an sp<sup>x</sup> orbital in the nodal plane of the π system; they are not stabilised by conjugation, so C–H BDEs are large (vinyl 111, phenyl 112, ethynyl 133 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) and the radicals are very reactive. The acyl radical R–C•=O is a σ radical, bent at carbon; it loses CO (decarbonylation) when R• is stable (e.g. R = t-Bu, benzyl).",
          "<b>EPR (ESR) spectroscopy</b> detects unpaired electrons. The hyperfine splitting a<sub>H</sub> from <i>n</i> equivalent protons gives <i>n</i> + 1 lines of binomial intensity, and the magnitude is proportional to the spin density on the carbon bearing H (McConnell equation, a<sub>H</sub> = Q·ρ, Q ≈ −23 G). Data (approximate): CH<sub>3</sub>•: 1:3:3:1 quartet, a<sub>H</sub> ≈ 23 G; ethyl: α-H (2) ≈ 22 G and β-H (3) ≈ 27 G, giving a triplet of quartets (12 lines); benzyl: ortho (2) ≈ 5 G, meta (2) ≈ 1.8 G, para (1) ≈ 6 G, α (2) ≈ 16 G, showing the spin density is delocalised to ortho/para positions; allyl radical: terminal H 13–14 G, central H 4 G, with 3 sets of lines (2 + 2 + 1 H). The large β-H coupling is hyperconjugation (the C–H bond overlapping the SOMO) and depends on the dihedral angle as cos<sup>2</sup>θ (Heller–McConnell), which allows conformational analysis of radicals.",
          "<b>Spin trapping.</b> Very reactive radicals are detected by adding them to a nitrone or nitroso compound (PBN, DMPO), giving a persistent nitroxide whose EPR spectrum identifies the trapped radical.",
          "<b>CIDNP</b> (chemically induced dynamic nuclear polarisation): NMR signals appear in enhanced absorption or emission during a radical-pair reaction, which is proof of a radical-pair mechanism (e.g. decomposition of benzoyl peroxide, Stevens and Wittig rearrangements)."])
    B.h2("9.12  Stability of radicals: bond dissociation energies and radical stabilisation energy")
    B.p("Radical stability is measured thermodynamically by the C–H bond dissociation energy of its parent, or by the <b>radical stabilisation energy RSE</b> = D(CH<sub>3</sub>–H) − D(R–H). The values below are recalled from standard tables (kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>); they are accurate to a few units and are used for <i>ranking</i>.")
    B.table(["Radical", "D(R–H)", "RSE", "Origin of the stabilisation"],
        [["•CH<sub>3</sub>", "105", "0", "reference"],
         ["CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>• (1°)", "101", "4", "hyperconjugation with 3 β C–H"],
         ["(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH• (2°)", "98.5", "6.5", "6 β C–H"],
         ["(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C• (3°)", "96.5", "8.5", "9 β C–H, and release of strain on moving from sp<sup>3</sup> to sp<sup>2</sup>"],
         ["CH<sub>2</sub>=CH–CH<sub>2</sub>•", "88", "17", "allylic resonance (two equivalent forms), loses ≈ 13 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> from the π-delocalisation net of rotation barrier"],
         ["C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>•", "89.8", "15", "resonance with benzene ring (four forms), partial loss of aromatic π energy"],
         ["(C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH•", "≈ 84", "21", "two aryl groups (nonplanar)"],
         ["(C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C•", "≈ 81", "24", "three aryl groups, sterically twisted propeller; persists in solution, dimerises through the para position of one ring (Gomberg dimer)"],
         ["•CH<sub>2</sub>OH, •CH<sub>2</sub>OCH<sub>3</sub>", "≈ 96", "9–10", "n(O) → SOMO donation (3-electron 2-orbital)"],
         ["•CH<sub>2</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub>, •CH<sub>2</sub>CN, •CH<sub>2</sub>COOR", "≈ 92–94", "11–13", "SOMO conjugation with π* (C=O, C≡N)"],
         ["C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>• (σ)", "112", "−7", "none; destabilised by high s-character"],
         ["CH<sub>2</sub>=CH• (σ)", "111", "−6", "none"],
         ["HC≡C•", "133", "−28", "50 % s-character"],
         ["CH<sub>3</sub>C(=O)• (acyl)", "≈ 89", "≈ 16", "partly carbonyl-conjugated σ/π hybrid"]],
        widths=[28, 12, 10, 50])
    B.ul(["<b>The radical is not a charge, so both donors and acceptors stabilise it.</b> The <i>captodative</i> effect: a carbon carrying simultaneously an electron donor (OR, NR<sub>2</sub>) and an acceptor (CN, COR) is more stable than the sum of the individual effects (e.g. •C(CN)(OR)H), as both SOMO interactions apply (donor lone pair → SOMO, SOMO → acceptor π*).",
          "<b>Why RSE for benzyl is lower than for allyl</b> despite more resonance forms: delocalisation into the ring costs some aromatic stabilisation energy; the experimental difference between the BDEs is small (≈ 2 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) and a JEE question that asks for the order normally expects <i>benzyl &gt; allyl</i> by the number of resonance structures (benzyl: 5 including the radical form; allyl: 2). ⚑ The data order is allyl ≈ benzyl; tell which the exam uses.",
          "<b>Radical versus cation versus anion.</b> Alkyl substituents stabilise radicals (weakly) and cations (strongly) and <i>destabilise</i> anions. Resonance with π systems stabilises all three. The order of stability of alkyl radicals (3° &gt; 2° &gt; 1° &gt; CH<sub>3</sub>) is the same as for carbocations, but the spread is a quarter as large: ΔD = 8.5 versus ΔHCIA ≈ 40 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> for cations.",
          "<b>Steric effects.</b> Bulky groups (t-butyl) raise radical stability by kinetic persistence (no combination): the 2,4,6-tri-t-butylphenoxyl radical, TEMPO (a nitroxide), DPPH, galvinoxyl and the triarylmethyls are persistent or even indefinitely stable. A stable radical can be bottled; a persistent radical lives long because its decay by self-reaction is slow; a transient radical decays at the diffusion limit."])
    B.h3("Stable and persistent radicals")
    B.table(["Radical", "Structure and why it persists", "Use"],
        [["Triphenylmethyl (trityl)", "Ph<sub>3</sub>C•; steric hindrance to dimerisation at the central carbon; the dimer (Gomberg 1900) is a quinoid compound formed by attack at the para-carbon of one ring (≈ 2 % radical at 0.1 M, 25 °C, in benzene)", "first radical ever observed; yellow, reversible with O<sub>2</sub> (gives peroxide)"],
         ["Nitroxides: TEMPO", "2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidin-1-oxyl: N–O• with three-electron N∴O bond and no α-hydrogens (no disproportionation)", "oxidation catalyst (alcohol → carbonyl), spin label, radical trap in living polymerisation (NMP)"],
         ["DPPH", "2,2-diphenyl-1-picrylhydrazyl: spin delocalised over the hydrazyl N–N and the picryl ring", "radical scavenger assay; EPR standard (g = 2.0036)"],
         ["Galvinoxyl; Fremy’s salt", "O-centred radicals delocalised through quinone methide; (KSO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NO•", "inhibitor, oxidant (Teuber reaction)"],
         ["2,4,6-tri-t-butylphenoxyl", "ArO•; the t-butyl groups block ortho/para coupling; green, stable for days", "model of antioxidant radical"],
         ["Molecular oxygen", "triplet O<sub>2</sub> (ground state is a diradical •O–O•)", "traps carbon radicals at diffusion rate; inhibits radical chains and starts autoxidation"],
         ["NO, NO<sub>2</sub>", "odd-electron molecules; NO is a persistent radical trap", "atmospheric chemistry, nitrosation"]],
        widths=[22, 50, 28])
    B.h2("9.13  Reactivity and selectivity: the Hammond postulate for hydrogen abstraction")
    B.p("For X• + R–H → X–H + R•, the transition state is <i>early</i> (reactant-like) when the step is strongly exothermic and <i>late</i> (product-like) when it is endothermic (Hammond). A late TS has substantial radical character on carbon, so the stability of R• is felt strongly in the activation energy; an early TS barely feels it. Hence the more reactive reagent is the less selective.")
    B.table(["Halogen radical", "ΔH (kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) for CH<sub>4</sub>", "E<sub>a</sub> for CH<sub>4</sub>", "TS", "Relative rate per H: 1° : 2° : 3°", "Selectivity"],
        [["F•", "−32", "≈ 1", "very early", "1 : 1.2 : 1.4  (25 °C)", "almost none"],
         ["Cl•", "+1", "3.8", "early", "1 : 3.8 : 5.0  (25 °C)", "low"],
         ["Br•", "+17", "18", "late", "1 : 82 : 1640  (127 °C)", "very high"],
         ["I•", "+34", "≈ 34", "very late", "H abstraction does not occur", "—"]],
        widths=[14, 20, 12, 12, 26, 16])
    B.ul(["<b>Product distribution</b> = (number of H of that type) × (relative reactivity) / Σ. Propane + Cl<sub>2</sub>/hν: 6 × 1 = 6 (1-chloro) versus 2 × 3.8 = 7.6 (2-chloro) → 44 : 56. Isobutane + Cl<sub>2</sub>: 9 × 1 = 9 versus 1 × 5.0 = 5 → 64 : 36 (primary chloride is the major product). Isobutane + Br<sub>2</sub>: 9 × 1 versus 1 × 1640 → &gt; 99 % tert-butyl bromide. Butane + Br<sub>2</sub>: 6 × 1 versus 4 × 82 → 98 % 2-bromobutane.",
          "<b>Bromine is selective because the first step is endothermic:</b> the TS resembles R•, so the energy difference between 1°, 2° and 3° radicals (≈ 2–3 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) appears almost fully in ΔE<sub>a</sub>.",
          "<b>Chlorine is less selective for two reasons:</b> an early TS (low E<sub>a</sub>) and a high collision-controlled rate. In solvents that complex Cl• (benzene, CS<sub>2</sub>, alkenes) the Cl•–π complex is more selective (3° : 1° rises from 5 to 50 in 8 M benzene).",
          "<b>Polar effects.</b> The TS has charge-transfer character: Cl• (electrophilic) prefers electron-rich C–H and avoids H on carbons bearing EWG: chlorination of CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COCl gives mainly the β-chloride (CH<sub>3</sub>CHClCOCl is minor), and chlorination of 1-chloropropane gives 1,2-, 1,3- and 1,1-dichloro products in a ratio 50 : 30 : 10 : 10 (approx.), not statistical. Bromine radical is even more discriminating and reacts with toluene at the benzylic position at least 10<sup>5</sup> times faster than with methane.",
          "<b>Isotope effects.</b> k<sub>H</sub>/k<sub>D</sub> for H abstraction: Cl• ≈ 1.3–1.5 (small, early TS, little C–H stretching), Br• ≈ 4.6 (large, late TS), but the free energy of the abstracted C–H dominates. A competition experiment between C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> and C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CD<sub>3</sub> brominated with NBS gives k<sub>H</sub>/k<sub>D</sub> = 4.9, proving C–H cleavage is rate-determining and Br• (not Br<sub>2</sub> ionic path) is the chain carrier.",
          "<b>Why fluorination and iodination are not used:</b> F<sub>2</sub> reacts violently (the C–H abstraction is −32 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>, and homolysis of F<sub>2</sub> needs only 38), leading to C–C cleavage; with I<sub>2</sub> the second step is fine but the first (I• + R–H → HI + R•) is +34 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> uphill, and HI reduces the alkyl iodide back to RH (RI + HI ⇌ RH + I<sub>2</sub>), so iodoalkanes are made by halide exchange (Finkelstein) rather than radical iodination."])
    B.h2("9.14  Radical addition to C=C; regiochemistry, polarity and Baldwin–Beckwith rules")
    B.ul(["<b>Regiochemistry.</b> A radical X• adds to the less hindered carbon of a C=C so that the new radical is the more stable one (benzylic, allylic, tertiary, α to C=O). Addition to styrene occurs at CH<sub>2</sub>; addition to methyl acrylate at CH<sub>2</sub> (giving the α-ester radical); to 1,3-butadiene at C-1 (giving the allylic radical).",
          "<b>Polar effects in radical addition.</b> Nucleophilic radicals (alkyl, α-hydroxyalkyl, α-aminoalkyl) add faster to electron-poor alkenes (acrylonitrile, acrylates), electrophilic radicals (•CF<sub>3</sub>, •CCl<sub>3</sub>, Br•, RS•, malonyl radicals) add faster to electron-rich alkenes (vinyl ethers, enamines). This ‘polar effect’ explains copolymerisation behaviour: styrene (donor) and maleic anhydride (acceptor) copolymerise in strict alternation.",
          "<b>Thiyl, bromine, and CCl<sub>3</sub> addition</b>: Br• and RS• add reversibly; the equilibrium allows cis→trans isomerisation of alkenes by catalytic Br• or RS• (add–rotate–eliminate), the basis of the trans-fat formation and the Z/E isomerisation of oleic to elaidic acid. CBrCl<sub>3</sub> and CCl<sub>4</sub> add across alkenes by chain: Cl<sub>3</sub>C• + CH<sub>2</sub>=CHR → Cl<sub>3</sub>C–CH<sub>2</sub>–C•HR, which abstracts Br (from CBrCl<sub>3</sub>) to give Cl<sub>3</sub>C–CH<sub>2</sub>–CHBr–R (Kharasch addition).",
          "<b>Anti-Markovnikov addition of HBr (peroxide effect).</b> Only HBr adds by the radical chain: HCl is too strong an H-donor bond (103 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>; the step R• + H–Cl is endothermic by ≈ 4–7); HI adds to the alkene, but I• then adds reversibly and is too unreactive (I• + alkene is endothermic, ΔH ≈ +4): the chain does not propagate. HBr is the only hydrogen halide for which both propagation steps are exothermic or thermoneutral (Br• + alkene ≈ −5, R• + HBr ≈ −8 to −10 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>).",
          "<b>Cyclisation of ω-alkenyl radicals (Baldwin–Beckwith).</b> The 5-hexenyl radical cyclises 5-<i>exo</i> (k ≈ 2 × 10<sup>5</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> at 25 °C) in preference to 6-<i>endo</i> (k ≈ 4 × 10<sup>3</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>), 50 : 1, though the six-membered radical is the more stable; this is stereoelectronic: the chair-like TS of the 5-exo path places the SOMO in better overlap with π* (Beckwith–Houk model). Methyl substituents at C-5 reverse the selectivity (6-endo) by hindering the exo attack. 4-pentenyl (4-exo) and 3-butenyl cyclisations are unfavourable; 6-heptenyl gives 6-exo, 7-endo slowly. Cyclisation onto C≡C and C=O is also known (Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH-mediated radical cyclisations used in natural-product synthesis).",
          "<b>Intermolecular versus intramolecular competition</b> is controlled by concentration of the H-donor: slow addition of Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH (syringe pump, 0.02 M) favours cyclisation; high [Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH] gives reduction without ring closure. This is the basis of using a ‘radical clock’ cyclisation to time a reaction."])
    B.h2("9.15  Radical chain reactions: initiation, propagation, termination, inhibition")
    B.table(["Stage", "Description", "Examples"],
        [["Initiation", "formation of the first radicals, usually by homolysis of a weak bond", "Cl–Cl (hν, 58); RO–OR (≈ 35); PhCO–O–O–COPh (≈ 30); AIBN (N–C, 31, loses N<sub>2</sub>); Br–Br; N–Br of NBS (via HBr + NBS → Br<sub>2</sub>); photolysis of ketones (Norrish I, II); Fenton: Fe<sup>2+</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> → Fe<sup>3+</sup> + HO<sup>−</sup> + HO•"],
         ["Propagation", "each step consumes one radical and produces another; the number of radical produced is conserved", "X• + R–H → HX + R•; R• + X<sub>2</sub> → RX + X•. The <b>kinetic chain length</b> (steps per initiation) is 10<sup>3</sup>–10<sup>6</sup>; quantum yield Φ = molecules of product per photon is then ≫ 1"],
         ["Termination", "two radicals combine or disproportionate", "R• + R• → R–R; 2 CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>• → CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> (disproportionation); radical + wall; radical + inhibitor"],
         ["Inhibition / retardation", "a species that reacts with chain carriers to give unreactive radicals; induction period", "O<sub>2</sub>, I<sub>2</sub>, quinones, phenols (BHT, α-tocopherol), nitroxides, DPPH, NO"]],
        widths=[16, 40, 44])
    B.ul(["<b>Test for a chain.</b> (i) quantum yield Φ ≫ 1; (ii) rate is first order in [initiator]<sup>1/2</sup> (because termination is second order in radicals: R<sub>i</sub> = 2k<sub>t</sub>[R•]<sup>2</sup>); (iii) strongly inhibited by traces of O<sub>2</sub>, hydroquinone or galvinoxyl; (iv) accelerated by hν or initiator; (v) insensitive to polar solvents (a radical rather than ionic mechanism).",
          "<b>Why only a small amount of peroxide is needed:</b> each initiating radical produces a long chain. Peroxide is used catalytically (0.1–1 mol %).",
          "<b>Why radical addition to alkenes is not driven by hν alone for HBr:</b> thermochemistry of the two propagation steps decides whether a chain is possible (HF, HCl, HI fail).",
          "<b>Autoxidation</b> (aerial oxidation) is a radical chain: R• + O<sub>2</sub> → ROO• (k ≈ 10<sup>9</sup> M<sup>−1</sup>s<sup>−1</sup>); ROO• + R–H → ROOH + R• (the rate-determining step, selective for weak C–H bonds: benzylic, allylic, tertiary, α to O). Examples: cumene → cumene hydroperoxide (industrial phenol/acetone synthesis, the Hock process), peroxide formation in ethers and THF (isopropyl ether is dangerous), drying of linseed oil, rancidity of fats (allylic C–H of polyunsaturated acids), ageing of rubber. Antioxidants (BHT, vitamin E) donate H• to ROO•, converting it to an unreactive ArO•."])
    B.h2("9.16  Radical polymerisation")
    B.p("Vinyl monomers CH<sub>2</sub>=CHX polymerise by a radical chain: <b>initiation</b> (In• + CH<sub>2</sub>=CHX → In–CH<sub>2</sub>–C•HX), <b>propagation</b> (head-to-tail addition to the less substituted carbon, so the growing radical is always α to X) and <b>termination</b> (combination or disproportionation, or chain transfer to monomer, solvent or polymer). Termination by combination gives chains twice the kinetic chain length.")
    B.table(["Polymer", "Monomer", "Notes"],
        [["Polyethylene (LDPE)", "CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub>", "high pressure (1000–3000 atm), 200 °C, trace O<sub>2</sub> or peroxide; branching through 1,5-backbiting (intramolecular H transfer via a six-membered TS) gives short-chain branches (butyl)"],
         ["Polystyrene", "PhCH=CH<sub>2</sub>", "AIBN or BPO, 60–80 °C; atactic"],
         ["PVC", "CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCl", "chain transfer to monomer dominates"],
         ["PAN (Orlon, Acrilan)", "CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCN", ""],
         ["PMMA (Perspex, Lucite)", "CH<sub>2</sub>=C(CH<sub>3</sub>)COOCH<sub>3</sub>", "termination mostly by disproportionation; Trommsdorff (gel) effect"],
         ["Teflon (PTFE)", "CF<sub>2</sub>=CF<sub>2</sub>", "persulfate initiator, aqueous"],
         ["Neoprene", "CH<sub>2</sub>=CCl–CH=CH<sub>2</sub> (chloroprene)", "1,4-addition through allylic radical"],
         ["Buna-S (SBR)", "butadiene + styrene (3 : 1)", "copolymer, emulsion"],
         ["Buna-N", "butadiene + acrylonitrile", "oil-resistant rubber"],
         ["Polyvinyl acetate", "CH<sub>2</sub>=CHOCOCH<sub>3</sub>", "hydrolysed to PVA"]],
        widths=[22, 28, 50])
    B.ul(["<b>Why polymerisation is thermodynamically allowed:</b> a C=C π bond (≈ 63 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) is replaced by a σ bond (≈ 88), ΔH ≈ −20 to −25 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> per addition; ΔS is negative, so there is a ceiling temperature (T<sub>c</sub> = ΔH/ΔS), above which depolymerisation wins (α-methylstyrene: T<sub>c</sub> = 61 °C).",
          "<b>Head-to-tail regiochemistry</b> is due to the preference for attack at the unsubstituted CH<sub>2</sub> (steric access and radical stability).",
          "<b>Inhibitors and retarders</b> (hydroquinone, BHT) are added to stabilise monomers during storage; O<sub>2</sub> is the most common inhibitor in practice.",
          "<b>Controlled radical polymerisation:</b> ATRP (R–X + Cu(I) ⇌ R• + Cu(II)X), RAFT, NMP (TEMPO-capped chain ends) keep the radical concentration very low with a dynamic equilibrium between active radicals and dormant species, giving narrow molecular-weight distributions."])
    B.h2("9.17  Fragmentation and rearrangement of radicals")
    B.table(["Process", "Equation", "Comment"],
        [["β-Scission (C–C)", "R–CH<sub>2</sub>–C•H<sub>2</sub> ⇌ R• + CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub>", "reverse of radical addition; important in thermal cracking (~500 °C) and in the combustion and pyrolysis of hydrocarbons; the thermodynamically favoured direction depends on ΔH (about +21 for ethyl → ethene + H•)"],
         ["Decarboxylation of acyloxy", "RCOO• → R• + CO<sub>2</sub>", "very fast for alkanoyloxy (k ≈ 10<sup>9</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>), slow for aroyloxy (10<sup>6</sup>; benzoyloxy partly abstracts or adds to arenes)"],
         ["Decarbonylation", "RC•=O → R• + CO", "easy when R• is stabilised (tertiary, benzylic); the aldehyde chain reaction R–CHO → R–H + CO"],
         ["Alkoxy radical β-scission", "R<sub>3</sub>C–O• → R• + R<sub>2</sub>C=O", "releases the most stable alkyl radical; alkoxy radical from tert-butyl hydroperoxide gives CH<sub>3</sub>• + acetone; used for ring expansion and the Barton nitrite photolysis"],
         ["1,5-Hydrogen atom transfer (1,5-HAT)", "RO• ... H–C<sub>δ</sub> via a six-membered chair-like TS", "Barton nitrite reaction (nitrite ester photolysis; δ-C–H → oxime), Hofmann–Löffler–Freytag (N-chloroamine, δ-H, pyrrolidine), 1,5-backbiting of PE chains; 1,2-H shifts of radicals do not occur (cf. carbocations)"],
         ["1,2-Aryl migration (neophyl)", "PhC(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>• → •C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Ph", "via a spirocyclohexadienyl radical (3-membered bridged); 1,2-vinyl, 1,2-acyl (Dowd–Beckwith), 1,2-Cl, Br shifts (via bridged radicals) also happen; alkyl and H do not"],
         ["Ring opening of cyclopropylcarbinyl", "•CH<sub>2</sub>–cPr → CH<sub>2</sub>=CH–CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>•", "k ≈ 10<sup>8</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> at 25 °C: ‘radical clock’; cyclobutylcarbinyl 5 × 10<sup>3</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>"],
         ["Dowd–Beckwith ring expansion", "ring-bearing CH<sub>2</sub>• attached to a ketone α-carbon; adds to C=O then ring-opens", "a ring-expansion of β-ketoesters by one or more CH<sub>2</sub> via alkoxy radical intermediate (3-exo attack on C=O)"]],
        widths=[22, 34, 44])
    B.h2("9.18  Other named radical reactions and their key features")
    B.table(["Reaction", "Reagents / conditions", "Radical intermediate and outcome"],
        [["Wohl–Ziegler bromination", "NBS, CCl<sub>4</sub>, hν or peroxide", "Br• abstracts allylic/benzylic H; R• + Br<sub>2</sub>; Br<sub>2</sub> supplied in tiny concentration from NBS + HBr; allylic transposition for unsymmetrical alkenes"],
         ["Hunsdiecker–Borodin", "RCOOAg + Br<sub>2</sub>, CCl<sub>4</sub>", "RCOOBr → RCOO• → R• + CO<sub>2</sub>; R–Br with one carbon less; fails for tertiary acids in some cases and requires dry salt"],
         ["Kolbe electrolysis", "RCOO<sup>−</sup>, anode (Pt), MeOH, high current density", "RCOO• → R•; 2R• → R–R; mixed Kolbe gives all three couplings"],
         ["Barton decarboxylation", "N-acyloxy-2-thiopyridone ester + Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH, hν", "R• formed by radical-chain fragmentation; reduced to R–H"],
         ["Sandmeyer", "ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> + CuCl/CuBr/CuCN", "single-electron transfer Cu(I) → ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> gives Ar• + N<sub>2</sub>; Ar• + CuX<sub>2</sub> → ArX + CuX"],
         ["Gomberg–Bachmann", "ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> + Ar′H, NaOH", "Ar• adds to Ar′H (ortho/para to substituents, almost regardless of electronic effect), cyclohexadienyl radical, −H•: biaryls in low yield"],
         ["Pschorr cyclisation", "o-substituted diazonium of a stilbene/biarylamine, Cu", "intramolecular radical arylation → phenanthrene/fluorenone"],
         ["Kharasch addition", "CCl<sub>4</sub>, CBrCl<sub>3</sub>, ICH<sub>2</sub>COOR + alkene, peroxide", "R• adds to alkene; adduct radical abstracts X"],
         ["Anti-Markovnikov HBr", "HBr, ROOR", "Br• chain (9.14)"],
         ["Pinacol coupling (Mg/Hg, SmI<sub>2</sub>)", "ketone + 2e<sup>−</sup>", "ketyl radical anion dimerises → 1,2-diol (see Part 13)"],
         ["Acyloin condensation", "RCOOR′ + Na, toluene reflux, then TMSCl", "ester radical anion couples to α-diketone → enediolate → α-hydroxy ketone; high yield for ring closure of medium and large rings"],
         ["Birch reduction", "arene + Na/NH<sub>3</sub>(l), ROH", "arene radical anion, protonation, second electron, protonation: 1,4-cyclohexadiene (Part 13)"],
         ["Hofmann–Löffler–Freytag", "N-chloroamine, H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, hν; then base", "aminium radical, 1,5-HAT, Cl abstraction, S<sub>N</sub>2 ring closure: pyrrolidine"],
         ["Barton nitrite photolysis", "R–CH<sub>2</sub>–O–N=O, hν", "RO• 1,5-HAT, trapped by NO; δ-nitroso → oxime; used for functionalising the unactivated C-18 methyl of steroids (aldosterone synthesis)"],
         ["Fenton / Haber–Weiss", "Fe<sup>2+</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>", "HO• (very reactive, abstracts any C–H, adds to arenes: hydroxylation); oxidative damage in biology"],
         ["Autoxidation, Hock process", "cumene + O<sub>2</sub>", "cumyl radical → cumyl-OO• → cumene hydroperoxide; acid-catalysed rearrangement (Part 7 type, aryl migration to O) gives phenol + acetone"]],
        widths=[20, 28, 52])
    B.h2("9.19  Stereochemistry of radical reactions")
    B.ul(["Radical formation at a stereocentre usually gives a planar (or rapidly inverting) radical, so the product is racemic: bromination of (S)-3-methylhexane at C-3 gives racemic 3-bromo-3-methylhexane.",
          "Radical addition to a cycloalkene typically gives <i>trans</i>-addition products because the second group is delivered anti to the first group already on the ring, e.g. HBr/peroxide addition to 1-methylcyclohexene gives mainly trans-1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane ([Br•] adds to C-2 equatorial first; H-transfer occurs from the opposite face).",
          "Radical cyclisation of 2-substituted 5-hexenyl radicals gives mainly cis-1,3 / trans-1,2 product (Beckwith chair rule): 1- or 3-substituents give cis-, 2- or 4-substituents give trans-disubstituted cyclopentanes.",
          "In a cage, a radical pair can recombine with retention of configuration (Wittig [1,2], Stevens, Barton nitrite) when the pair is held in the solvent cage faster than it diffuses apart.",
          "A radical α to an existing stereocentre is attacked from the less hindered face (1,2-asymmetric induction), as in the Giese addition to chiral radicals; the selectivity improves at low temperature."])
    B.warn("<b>Typical errors in radical questions.</b> (1) Writing H or alkyl 1,2-shifts for radicals. (2) Giving HBr anti-Markovnikov addition for HCl or HI. (3) Counting each H as equally reactive in chlorination of an unsymmetrical alkane; use (number of H) × (relative rate). "
           "(4) Treating the radical as charged and applying −I/+I arguments: use bond energies and delocalisation. (5) Forgetting that allyl radicals give both regioisomers (NBS on 1-heptene gives 3-bromo- and 1-bromo-hept-2-ene). "
           "(6) Using AIBN/peroxide as a reactant: it is an initiator (catalytic). (7) Assuming a radical chain is operating in the presence of O<sub>2</sub>/quinone; those inhibit it. (8) Treating the Sandmeyer reaction as nucleophilic substitution (it is radical-nucleophilic, SRN1-like, with SET from Cu(I)).")


def mechanisms(B):
    c = []
    c.append(ex("9.12", "Initiation: homolysis of AIBN with loss of N<sub>2</sub>",
        "[CH3:1][C:2]([CH3:3])([C:4]#[N:5])[N:6]=[N:7][C:8]([CH3:9])([CH3:10])[C:11]#[N:12]",
        [St([R(('b', 2, 6, 2), ('nb', 6, 7), 0.4), R(('b', 8, 7, 8), ('nb', 6, 7), -0.4)], "Δ or hν", rad=True, expect="N#N.C[C](C)C#N.C[C](C)C#N")],
        "N#N.C[C](C)C#N.C[C](C)C#N",
        "Both C–N bonds break; the two electrons that would remain on N each pair to give the very strong N≡N bond (226 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>), which is why AIBN decomposes readily at 60–80 °C. One electron of each broken σ bond stays with its carbon, giving two 2-cyano-2-propyl radicals. Each fish-hook ends at the same N–N bond: two arrows = one extra bond order.", width=680))
    c.append(ex("9.13", "Decarboxylation of the benzoyloxy radical (from benzoyl peroxide)",
        "[O:1]([C:2](=[O:3])" + Ph(10) + ")[O:4][C:5](=[O:6])" + Ph(20),
        [St([R(('b', 1, 4), ('rad', 1), 0.4), R(('b', 1, 4), ('rad', 4), -0.4)], "Δ", rad=True, expect="[O]C(=O)c1ccccc1.[O]C(=O)c1ccccc1")],
        "[O]C(=O)c1ccccc1.[O]C(=O)c1ccccc1",
        "The weak O–O bond (≈ 30 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) breaks homolytically. Benzoyloxy radicals lose CO<sub>2</sub> only slowly (k ≈ 10<sup>6</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>) so many add to the monomer or arene before decarboxylating, unlike alkanoyloxy radicals, which lose CO<sub>2</sub> within nanoseconds.", width=680))
    c.append(ex("9.14", "5-Exo cyclisation of the 5-hexenyl radical",
        "[CH2:1][CH2:2][CH2:3][CH2:4][CH:5]=[CH2:6]",
        [St([R(('rad', 1), ('nb', 1, 5), 0.4), R(('b', 5, 6, 6), ('nb', 1, 5), -0.4)], "5-exo-trig", rad=True, expect="[CH2]C1CCCC1")],
        "[CH2]C1CCCC1",
        "The SOMO attacks the nearer carbon of the alkene (C-5), forming a five-membered ring; the π electron pair splits, one forming the new σ bond and the other left on C-6 (primary, exocyclic cyclopentylmethyl radical). The product radical is less stable than the 6-endo secondary alternative, yet 5-exo is faster by a factor of ≈ 50 (stereoelectronic control, Beckwith). With Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH the radical is reduced to methylcyclopentane.", width=680))
    c.append(ex("9.15", "1,5-Hydrogen atom transfer in an alkoxy radical (Barton / Hofmann–Löffler type)",
        "[O:1][CH2:2][CH2:3][CH2:4][CH2:5][H:6]",
        [St([R(('rad', 1), ('nb', 1, 6), 0.4), R(('b', 5, 6, 5), ('nb', 1, 6), -0.4)], "1,5-HAT", rad=True, expect="OCCC[CH2]")],
        "OCCC[CH2]",
        "The oxygen radical reaches the δ-hydrogen through a six-membered, chair-like transition state; the O–H bond formed (105 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) is stronger than the C–H bond broken (99), so the step is exothermic by ≈ 6 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>, with a very low barrier (k ≈ 10<sup>7</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>). The new carbon radical is trapped by NO (Barton) or Cl (Hofmann–Löffler–Freytag).", width=680))
    c.append(ex("9.16", "Autoxidation: carbon radical traps O<sub>2</sub>",
        "[CH3:1].[O:2]=[O:3]",
        [St([R(('rad', 1), ('nb', 1, 2), 0.4), R(('b', 2, 3, 3), ('nb', 1, 2), -0.4)], "k ≈ 10^9 M^−1 s^−1", rad=True, expect="CO[O]")],
        "CO[O]",
        "Triplet O<sub>2</sub> is a diradical, so it adds to a carbon radical at nearly the diffusion-controlled rate to give the peroxyl radical R–O–O•. The peroxyl radical then abstracts H from another molecule of R–H (slow, rate-determining) to give R–OOH and propagate the chain. This is why O<sub>2</sub> inhibits other radical chains (it traps R• faster than R• can react with the substrate) and why ethers, aldehydes and alkenes slowly oxidise in air.", width=680))
    c.append(ex("9.17", "Reduction by chain transfer: alkyl radical + thiol (RS–H) in radical polymerisation",
        "[CH2:1][CH:2]([CH3:3])" + Ph(4) + ".[S:20]([CH2:21][CH3:22])[H:23]",
        [St([R(('rad', 1), ('nb', 1, 23), 0.4), R(('b', 20, 23, 20), ('nb', 1, 23), -0.4)], "chain transfer", rad=True, expect="CC(C)c1ccccc1.CC[S]")],
        "CC(C)c1ccccc1.CC[S]",
        "Thiols have weak S–H bonds (≈ 87 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>), so they are excellent H-atom donors; a growing polymer radical abstracts the thiol hydrogen to give a dead chain and a thiyl radical, which re-initiates polymerisation. This ‘chain transfer’ reduces the average molecular weight (mercaptans are used as molecular-weight regulators in SBR production, e.g. dodecyl mercaptan).", width=680))
    c.append(ex("9.18", "β-Scission of the tert-butoxy radical",
        "[CH3:1][C:2]([CH3:3])([CH3:4])[O:5]",
        [St([R(('rad', 5), ('nb', 2, 5), 0.4), R(('b', 1, 2, 1), ('nb', 2, 5), -0.4)], "", rad=True, expect="CC(C)=O.[CH3]")],
        "CC(C)=O.[CH3]",
        "The oxygen radical electron and one electron of an adjacent C–C σ bond form the C=O π bond (the stronger C=O compensates for the lost C–C), while the other electron stays on CH<sub>3</sub>•. This competes with H-abstraction by t-BuO•: in non-H-donor solvents at higher temperatures it gives acetone and CH<sub>3</sub>• (the source of methane/ethane in t-BuOOt-Bu photolysis).", width=680))
    B.grid(c, 2)


def problems(B, q):
    q(17, "Chlorination of 2-methylbutane (C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>12</sub>) at 25 °C: relative rates per H are 1° : 2° : 3° = 1 : 3.8 : 5. Calculate the percentage of each monochloro constitutional isomer.",
      S([("1-chloro-2-methylbutane", "CCC(C)CCl"), ("2-chloro-2-methylbutane", "CCC(C)(C)Cl"), ("2-chloro-3-methylbutane", "CC(Cl)C(C)C"), ("1-chloro-3-methylbutane", "CC(C)CCCl")]),
      "27.8 % : 23.1 % : 35.2 % : 13.9 %",
      "Weighted counts: the two CH<sub>3</sub> on C-2 (6 H × 1 = 6) → 1-chloro-2-methylbutane; tertiary C-2 H (1 × 5 = 5) → 2-chloro-2-methylbutane; CH<sub>2</sub> (2 × 3.8 = 7.6) → 2-chloro-3-methylbutane; terminal CH<sub>3</sub> (3 × 1 = 3) → 1-chloro-3-methylbutane. Total 21.6; shares 6/21.6, 5/21.6, 7.6/21.6, 3/21.6.")
    q(18, "How many monochlorination products (including stereoisomers) can be formed from 2-methylbutane? How many constitutional isomers are chiral?",
      None, "4 constitutional isomers, 6 including stereoisomers; 2 of the constitutional isomers are chiral", "1-Chloro-2-methylbutane (stereocentre C-2: 2 enantiomers) and 2-chloro-3-methylbutane (stereocentre C-2: 2 enantiomers) are chiral; 2-chloro-2-methylbutane and 1-chloro-3-methylbutane are achiral. 2 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 6.")
    q(19, "At 127 °C, bromination of propane gives 2-bromopropane almost exclusively (97 %), while chlorination gives a 45 : 55 mixture. Explain with the Hammond postulate and give the energy diagrams in words.",
      None, "late versus early transition state", "Br• + R–H is endothermic (+17 for CH<sub>4</sub>; ≈ +7 for secondary C–H), so the TS resembles the alkyl radical and the 6.5-kcal gap between 1° and 2° radicals appears almost fully in ΔE<sub>a</sub> (≈ 2–3 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>). Cl• + R–H is exothermic for 1°/2° C–H, an early TS in which little radical character has developed, so the barrier difference is only ≈ 0.5 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>.")
    q(20, "Why does HBr but not HCl or HI add to propene by the radical chain? Give ΔH of the two propagation steps.",
      None, "only HBr has two exothermic propagation steps", "Approximate ΔH (kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) for X• + CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCH<sub>3</sub> and R• + HX: Cl: −22 and +3 (second step is endothermic); Br: −5 and −10 (both favourable); I: +4 and −26 (first step is endothermic, reversible, and the I• adds reversibly so no chain develops). Hence the ‘peroxide effect’ is restricted to HBr.")
    q(21, "NBS/CCl<sub>4</sub>/hν is used with each of: (a) cyclohexene, (b) toluene, (c) 1-pentene, (d) propene, (e) isobutane, (f) methylcyclohexane. Give the major product or products.",
      S([("a", "BrC1C=CCCC1"), ("b", "BrCc1ccccc1"), ("c", "C=CC(Br)CC; BrC=CC".replace("C=CC(Br)CC; BrC=CC", "C=CC(Br)CC")), ("d", "C=CCBr"), ("e", "CC(C)(C)Br"), ("f", "CC1(Br)CCCCC1")]), "(a) 3-bromocyclohexene; (b) benzyl bromide; (c) 3-bromo-1-pentene and 1-bromo-2-pentene (mixture); (d) allyl bromide; (e) t-butyl bromide; (f) 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane",
      "NBS brominates the weakest C–H: allylic, benzylic, or tertiary. For 1-pentene the allyl radical CH<sub>2</sub>=CH–C•H–CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> ↔ •CH<sub>2</sub>–CH=CH–CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> reacts at either end; the internal (more substituted, more stable) alkene 1-bromo-2-pentene predominates.")
    q(22, "In the Hunsdiecker reaction of silver 3-phenylpropanoate with Br<sub>2</sub>, the product is PhCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br. Write the radical steps and say how the product differs from the acid in carbon count.",
      None, "PhCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br: one carbon less than the acid", "RCOOAg + Br<sub>2</sub> → RCOOBr + AgBr; RCOOBr → RCOO• + Br• (initiation); RCOO• → R• + CO<sub>2</sub>; R• + RCOOBr → RBr + RCOO• (propagation). The acid PhCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COOH has 9 carbon, the bromide PhCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br has 8.")
    q(23, "Arrange the following radicals in increasing order of stability: (a) CH<sub>3</sub>OCH<sub>2</sub>•, (b) CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>•, (c) PhCH<sub>2</sub>•, (d) CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCH<sub>2</sub>•, (e) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C•, (f) •CH<sub>2</sub>CN.",
      S([("a", "CO[CH2]"), ("b", "C[CH2]"), ("c", "[CH2]c1ccccc1"), ("d", "C=C[CH2]"), ("e", "C[C](C)C"), ("f", "N#C[CH2]")]), "b &lt; e &lt; a &lt; f &lt; d ≈ c  (⚑ d vs c)", "RSE values: 4, 8.5, ≈ 9–10 (α-oxygen lone pair), ≈ 11–12 (cyano conjugation), 17, 15. Position of c versus d depends on the data source; most JEE keys put benzyl above allyl on resonance count.")
    q(24, "A hydrocarbon C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>14</sub> gives only two monochlorination products. Identify it and the ratio of the products if relative reactivities are 1 : 3.8 : 5.",
      S([("", "CC(C)C(C)C")]), "2,3-dimethylbutane; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CHCH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>: 12 primary H : 2 tertiary H → 12 × 1 : 2 × 5 = 12 : 10", "The two halves are identical, with 12 primary and 2 tertiary H. 1-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane : 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane = 54.5 % : 45.5 %. (2,2-dimethylbutane has three products; hexane has three.)")
    q(25, "Optically active (S)-1-phenylethyl chloride is treated with Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD / AIBN. What is the stereochemistry of the product PhCHDCH<sub>3</sub>?",
      None, "racemic", "Bu<sub>3</sub>Sn• abstracts Cl to give the planar (benzylic) radical; deuterium transfer from Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD occurs from either face with equal probability, giving a racemate.")
    q(26, "Account for the fact that trans-2-butene is formed from cis-2-butene on adding a trace of Br<sub>2</sub> and light (no net addition).",
      None, "Br• adds reversibly, the radical rotates, and Br• is eliminated", "Br• + cis-CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CHCH<sub>3</sub> → CH<sub>3</sub>C•H–CHBrCH<sub>3</sub>. Free rotation about the C–C single bond (≈ 1000 times faster than Br• loss) before Br• leaves gives the more stable trans isomer. Catalytic Br• or RS• thus equilibrates alkenes to the thermodynamic mixture.")
    q(27, "Predict the product of the addition of HBr to (a) propene and (b) 3,3,3-trifluoropropene, in the presence of peroxide; (c) in the dark with no peroxide. Explain the (b) result.",
      None, "(a) 1-bromopropane; (b) CF<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br; (c) 2-bromopropane for propene (ionic)", "(a) Br• adds at C-1, giving the secondary radical. (b) CF<sub>3</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub>: Br• adds at the CH<sub>2</sub> terminus to give CF<sub>3</sub>–C•H–CH<sub>2</sub>Br or at CH to give the primary radical; the CF<sub>3</sub> group destabilises the adjacent radical and cation (both polar and field effect), so the experimental product is CF<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br by either mechanism. (c) Ionic addition follows Markovnikov (2-bromopropane).")
    q(28, "The rate of polymerisation of styrene initiated by AIBN is proportional to [AIBN]<sup>1/2</sup> and [styrene]. Derive this from the steady-state hypothesis.",
      None, "R<sub>p</sub> = k<sub>p</sub>[M](f k<sub>d</sub>[I]/k<sub>t</sub>)<sup>1/2</sup>", "Steady-state: rate of initiation = rate of termination: 2fk<sub>d</sub>[I] = 2k<sub>t</sub>[M•]<sup>2</sup> ⇒ [M•] = (fk<sub>d</sub>[I]/k<sub>t</sub>)<sup>1/2</sup>. Rate of propagation = k<sub>p</sub>[M•][M]. Substituting gives R<sub>p</sub> ∝ [M][I]<sup>1/2</sup>. The half-order in initiator is the signature of bimolecular radical termination.")
    q(29, "What is the average degree of polymerisation if the kinetic chain length is 500 and termination is (a) entirely by combination, (b) entirely by disproportionation?",
      None, "(a) 1000; (b) 500", "Combination joins two kinetic chains in one macromolecule (DP = 2ν); disproportionation gives two molecules, each having one kinetic chain (DP = ν). PMMA terminates mainly by disproportionation; polystyrene by combination.")
    q(30, "Identify the product of benzoyl peroxide-initiated bromination of the following: toluene + Br<sub>2</sub> (hν), toluene + Br<sub>2</sub> (Fe), toluene + NBS. State what changes.",
      S([("hν", "BrCc1ccccc1"), ("Fe", "Cc1ccc(Br)cc1"), ("NBS", "BrCc1ccccc1")]), "benzyl bromide (side chain, radical); o- and p-bromotoluene (ring, ionic); benzyl bromide", "Light or radical initiator and non-polar solvent: radical substitution at the weakest C–H (benzylic, 89.8). A Lewis acid (FeBr<sub>3</sub>) generates Br<sup>+</sup> equivalents for electrophilic aromatic substitution.")
    q(31, "Write the products of the Kolbe electrolysis of (a) CH<sub>3</sub>COONa, (b) a 1 : 1 mixture of CH<sub>3</sub>COONa and CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COONa, (c) sodium succinate NaOOC–CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>–COONa.",
      S([("a", "CC"), ("b", "CC.CCC.CCCC"), ("c", "C=C")]), "(a) ethane; (b) ethane + propane + butane (1 : 2 : 1 by statistics); (c) ethene", "(a) 2 CH<sub>3</sub>• → C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>. (b) CH<sub>3</sub>•/C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>• couple randomly: 25 % CH<sub>3</sub>–CH<sub>3</sub>, 50 % CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> (propane), 25 % butane. (c) the diradical •CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>• forms ethene.")
    q(32, "How many monobromo products are possible on treating methylcyclohexane with Br<sub>2</sub>/hν? Which one will predominate and why?",
      None, "5 constitutional isomers; the tertiary bromide", "H types: CH<sub>3</sub> (1°), C-1 tertiary, C-2 (×2), C-3 (×2), C-4 → 5 constitutional isomers (counting C-2/C-6 and C-3/C-5 as pairs: five different environments in total). Br• discriminates 1600 : 82 : 1, so 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane forms almost exclusively.")
    q(33, "State and explain the product of the following: (a) CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub> + HBr (ROOR); (b) CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CHCH<sub>3</sub> + HBr (ROOR); (c) CH<sub>2</sub>=CH–CH=CH<sub>2</sub> + HBr (ROOR).",
      None, "(a) 1-bromobutane; (b) 2-bromobutane; (c) 1-bromo-2-butene (mainly) and 3-bromo-1-butene", "(a) Br• adds to the terminal CH<sub>2</sub>. (b) symmetric alkene gives only 2-bromobutane. (c) Br• adds at C-1 to give a resonance-stabilised allylic radical CH<sub>2</sub>Br–CH•–CH=CH<sub>2</sub> ↔ CH<sub>2</sub>Br–CH=CH–C•H<sub>2</sub>; H-transfer at either end gives 3-bromo-1-butene (kinetic, 1,2-adduct) and 1-bromo-2-butene (1,4, thermodynamic).")
    q(34, "A neophyl-type radical PhC(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>• rearranges, but CH<sub>3</sub>C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>• (neopentyl) does not. Why? Which other groups can migrate?",
      None, "Ph, vinyl, acyl, Cl/Br can; H and alkyl cannot", "Migration needs a low-energy bridged (three-membered) radical TS. For phenyl, the π system provides a stabilised spiro-cyclohexadienyl radical; for H or alkyl, the bridging TS has three centres and three electrons with no stabilisation, and the barrier is much higher than that for intermolecular abstraction. Neopentyl radical therefore abstracts H or fragments instead.")
    q(35, "Cumene (isopropylbenzene) is treated with O<sub>2</sub> at 100 °C with a trace of initiator, then with acid. Give the radical chain and the final products.",
      S([("", "CC(C)c1ccccc1"), ("", "CC(C)(OO)c1ccccc1"), ("", "Oc1ccccc1.CC(C)=O")]), "cumene hydroperoxide, then phenol + acetone (Hock process)", "In• + PhCH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> → PhC•(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> (tertiary benzylic); + O<sub>2</sub> → PhC(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>OO•; + cumene → PhC(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>OOH + cumyl radical. In acid, the hydroperoxide protonates, loses water as the phenyl migrates from C to O (cationic, Part 7-type shift) to give an oxocarbenium ion, which hydrolyses to phenol and acetone.")
    q(36, "Why is the termination step R• + R• → R–R rare in a propagating chain even though it has a rate constant of 10<sup>9</sup> M<sup>−1</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>?",
      None, "radical concentration is ≈ 10<sup>−8</sup> M", "Rate of termination = k[R•]<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>9</sup> × 10<sup>−16</sup> = 10<sup>−7</sup> M s<sup>−1</sup>, but propagation = k<sub>p</sub>[R•][M] ≈ (10<sup>2</sup>–10<sup>4</sup>) × 10<sup>−8</sup> × 1 M = 10<sup>−6</sup>–10<sup>−4</sup> M s<sup>−1</sup>. Propagation is first order in radical concentration and thus beats the second-order termination until [R•] is high (as in the Trommsdorff gel effect, where termination is slowed by high viscosity).")
    q(37, "Which of the following compounds is the best radical inhibitor? (a) benzene, (b) hydroquinone, (c) cyclohexane, (d) benzoyl peroxide, (e) cumene.",
      None, "(b) hydroquinone", "It donates an H atom (O–H BDE ≈ 80 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) to give a resonance-stabilised semiquinone radical that does not propagate; (d) is an initiator; (a), (c) have strong C–H bonds; (e) is easily autoxidised.")
    q(38, "In the HBr addition to 1-hexene catalysed by peroxide, the chain length is about 10<sup>4</sup>. How many HBr molecules are consumed per radical formed by peroxide? What changes if 1 % hydroquinone is added?",
      None, "10<sup>4</sup>; the reaction is inhibited (induction period)", "Each initiating radical starts a chain that adds ≈ 10<sup>4</sup> molecules before termination. Hydroquinone scavenges chain-carrying radicals, so the reaction does not start until it is consumed (induction period), and the product reverts to the ionic Markovnikov addition (2-bromohexane), a slower reaction.")
    q(39, "Name the stable free radical among (a) CH<sub>3</sub>•, (b) Ph<sub>3</sub>C•, (c) TEMPO, (d) t-BuO•, (e) Cl•, and say which of these gives a stable but reactive-solution radical (Gomberg 1900).",
      None, "(c) TEMPO is stable (isolable); (b) trityl persists in solution but dimerises on concentration", "(c) The four methyl groups block the α-positions, so there is neither disproportionation (no α-H) nor combination; the odd electron is delocalised over N–O. (b) persistent: steric hindrance prevents C–C coupling at the central carbon; the dimer is formed through the para position of one ring.")
    q(40, "Complete: (i) Ph–N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>−</sup> + Cu powder → ?; (ii) Ph–N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + NaOH → ?; (iii) CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COO<sup>−</sup> (anodic oxidation) → ?",
      S([("i", "Clc1ccccc1"), ("ii", "c1ccc(cc1)-c1ccccc1"), ("iii", "CCCC")]), "(i) chlorobenzene (Gattermann); (ii) biphenyl (Gomberg–Bachmann); (iii) butane (Kolbe)", "All three involve a radical (Ph• or CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>•). In (i) the Cu gives an electron to the diazonium and Cu(II)Cl<sub>2</sub> delivers Cl.")
    q(41, "How many different monobromo-alkenes can in principle form on allylic bromination of 1-methylcyclohexene with NBS (constitutional isomers only)? Which positions are allylic?",
      None, "6", "Allylic hydrogens are on the CH<sub>3</sub>, C-3 and C-6, giving three allylic radicals, each delocalised over two carbons: CH<sub>3</sub>-radical → 1-(bromomethyl)cyclohexene and 2-bromo-1-methylenecyclohexane; C-3 radical → 3-bromo-1-methylcyclohexene and 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohex-2-ene; C-6 radical → 6-bromo-1-methylcyclohexene and 2-bromo-1-methylcyclohex-1-ene-type isomer in which the double bond has moved to C-1=C-6. Six distinct constitutional isomers; the most stable (and so usually the major) are those retaining a trisubstituted double bond.")
    q(42, "The 5-hexenyl radical can cyclise in two ways. Name them, give the major product and the reason.",
      S([("", "C=CCCC[CH2]"), ("→", "[CH2]C1CCCC1")]), "5-exo (cyclopentylmethyl) major; 6-endo (cyclohexyl) minor", "5-exo-trig versus 6-endo-trig. The exo mode has better SOMO–π* overlap in a chair-like TS; the ratio is about 98 : 2 at 25 °C. A methyl at C-5 raises the exo barrier sterically and reverses the preference.")
    q(43, "1-Bromobicyclo[2.2.2]octane is used as a substrate for radical (Bu<sub>3</sub>SnH) reduction and as a substrate for S<sub>N</sub>1 solvolysis. Which proceeds easily, and why?",
      None, "radical reduction proceeds; S<sub>N</sub>1 does not", "A bridgehead carbocation cannot become planar and is highly destabilised, so S<sub>N</sub>1 is extremely slow. A radical only needs a modest degree of delocalisation and tolerates pyramidal geometry (radical inversion barriers are tiny), so the bridgehead radical forms readily from the bromide with Bu<sub>3</sub>Sn• (≈ 10<sup>5</sup> M<sup>−1</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>).")
    q(44, "Account for the ratio of products 1-bromo-1-phenylethane : 2-bromo-1-phenylethane = &gt; 99 : 1 in the NBS bromination of ethylbenzene.",
      S([("major", "CC(Br)c1ccccc1"), ("minor", "BrCCc1ccccc1")]), "benzylic H is 10<sup>3</sup> times more reactive than the terminal CH<sub>3</sub> H", "The secondary benzylic C–H (BDE ≈ 85.4) is 12–15 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> weaker than the terminal C–H, and Br• selects the weak bond.")
    q(45, "Using bond energies, show that Cl• + CH<sub>4</sub> → HCl + •CH<sub>3</sub> is nearly thermoneutral while Br• + CH<sub>4</sub> → HBr + •CH<sub>3</sub> is endothermic by 17 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>, and use this to explain why Br<sub>2</sub> is more selective than Cl<sub>2</sub>.",
      None, "ΔH(Cl) = 105 − 103 = +2 (+1); ΔH(Br) = 105 − 87.5 = +17.5", "ΔH = D(C–H) − D(H–X). The late (endothermic) TS of bromination resembles the alkyl radical product, so differences in radical stability are felt in the activation energy (Hammond), making bromination selective; the early TS of chlorination does not.")
    q(46, "(JEE Advanced style, integer) How many of the following will give the same single monohalogenation product upon radical bromination with one equivalent of Br<sub>2</sub>/hν: ethane, neopentane, cyclohexane, benzene, toluene (side-chain), 2,2,3,3-tetramethylbutane, adamantane?",
      None, "5  (ethane, neopentane, cyclohexane, toluene, 2,2,3,3-tetramethylbutane; adamantane gives two isomers, tertiary and secondary; benzene does not react by radical substitution)", "Ethane (1 type of H), neopentane (all 12 H equivalent), cyclohexane (12 equivalent H), toluene (only the benzylic H react), hexamethylethane (18 equivalent H). Adamantane has 12 secondary and 4 tertiary H, with Br• giving mainly tertiary (1-bromoadamantane).")
